{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>When a man… if they commit…</b> It says <b>to commit a trespass (לִמְעוֹל)</b> and does not say וּמָעֲלוּ מַעַל (and they committed a trespass). This is explained according to the words of our Sages of blessed memory (Sifrei; Pesikta; Bava Kamma 110a), who said that the verse speaks of one who deals falsely with his fellow's money and swears to him falsely. And even though this matter was already stated in the parashah of Vayikra, it was repeated to detail the laws that were newly introduced in it. Accordingly, the verse awakens [the understanding] that from the time of the sinful act itself — which is the robbery and the denial of money — he is already considered to commit a trespass against HaShem, for swearing in His name when they summon him to judgment, since were it not so, it would be merely a monetary accounting. And this is its saying <b>when they commit any of the sins of man</b> — which is robbing his fellow's money — from the time of the act itself, behold, he is already deemed to commit a trespass against HaShem." "<b>and that soul shall be guilty.</b> Because he sinned with an oath by HaShem, the defect takes effect in the soul. And the verse changed to speak in singular language, whereas everything before it and after it is in plural language. Before it, it says: <b>when they commit</b> etc.; after it: <b>and they shall confess</b>. And you will find that they said in the Gemara that when the verse makes you feel in such a manner [i.e., the shift to singular], it is a profound stirring. See what we have written on this in parashat Vayikra (Leviticus 7:21). Also, it was precise to say the word <b>that one (הַהוּא)</b> without need; the verse awakens [the understanding] that this blemish will leave an impression upon the source of the souls of the people of Israel, which is called נֶפֶשׁ. And in order that you not understand that it is speaking of the trespasser himself, therefore it said <b>that one</b> and did not say וְאָשְׁמָה נַפְשׁוֹ (and his soul shall be guilty). And see what is written in the Zohar (Acharei Mot 67a) in explanation of the verse ״נַפְשִׁי אִוִּיתִיךָ בַּלַּיְלָה״ (Isaiah 26:9), with precise analysis:"], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:06:19.050033442Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}